1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,549
Slow drift is the most expensive failure mode in

2
00:00:03,649 --> 00:00:08,948
manufacturing, because every single measurement of it looks acceptable.

3
00:00:09,033 --> 00:00:13,760
A Shewhart chart, limits at plus and minus three sigma, which

4
00:00:13,860 --> 00:00:18,509
buys one false alarm in three hundred and seventy subgroups.

5
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:22,388
The first twenty subgroups are noise around the target.

6
00:00:22,483 --> 00:00:26,082
Then the mean starts walking, 0.06 sigma per

7
00:00:26,182 --> 00:00:30,623
subgroup. Slow enough that no single measurement looks

8
00:00:30,723 --> 00:00:34,575
wrong, and the chart carries on saying nothing.

9
00:00:34,666 --> 00:00:40,705
The first violation lands at subgroup 64. That is 43 subgroups after the

10
00:00:40,805 --> 00:00:46,248
drift began, by which time the mean has moved 2.6 sigma and every

11
00:00:46,348 --> 00:00:51,534
part in between was made by a process nobody knew had changed.

12
00:00:51,633 --> 00:00:55,709
Each point was judged on its own and then forgotten.

13
00:00:55,809 --> 00:00:59,885
That is the whole weakness: the chart has no memory.

14
00:01:01,283 --> 00:01:06,575
Same process, same data, same false alarm budget — and a statistic

15
00:01:06,675 --> 00:01:12,295
that remembers. The faint points are the raw measurements from before.

16
00:01:12,383 --> 00:01:14,717
Each new subgroup gets a fifth of the

17
00:01:14,817 --> 00:01:17,875
weight and the running statistic keeps the rest.

18
00:01:17,966 --> 00:01:20,952
With lambda at zero point two, that is

19
00:01:21,052 --> 00:01:23,794
one part new and four parts memory.

20
00:01:23,883 --> 00:01:29,193
Its limits are not plus and minus three. They are calibrated

21
00:01:29,293 --> 00:01:34,423
by simulation until this chart cries wolf as rarely as the

22
00:01:34,523 --> 00:01:39,383
last one: one alarm in 370 quiet subgroups against 370.

23
00:01:39,483 --> 00:01:44,714
Watch the same 80 subgroups again. While the process is quiet the

24
00:01:44,814 --> 00:01:50,783
statistic wanders near zero, because new noise keeps cancelling old noise.

25
00:01:50,883 --> 00:01:56,056
Once the drift starts, the noise still cancels but the drift does

26
00:01:56,156 --> 00:02:00,599
not. It is the same direction every time, so it adds up.

27
00:02:00,683 --> 00:02:05,417
It crosses the limit at subgroup 34, with the mean only

28
00:02:05,517 --> 00:02:10,779
0.8 sigma off, and the raw measurement there sitting at +2.48

29
00:02:10,879 --> 00:02:15,438
sigma — a number no Shewhart chart would ever look at

30
00:02:15,538 --> 00:02:21,415
twice. The other chart waited until subgroup 64, 30 subgroups later.

31
00:02:21,500 --> 00:02:25,793
That is one drift. Run thousands of them and the average

32
00:02:25,893 --> 00:02:29,560
wait to detect a one sigma shift comes out at 44

33
00:02:29,660 --> 00:02:33,640
subgroups for the Shewhart rule and 10 for this one.

34
00:02:33,733 --> 00:02:39,419
Divide them: 4.4 times sooner, bought with no extra false alarms

35
00:02:39,519 --> 00:02:44,121
at all. That trade is the whole of detection theory.

